  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   The central idea is to represent the step function \\( h(x) \\) as a periodic extension of a triangle wave, optimized over its width and amplitude parameters. This approach leverages the **periodicity** and **analytical structure** of triangular functions, which often exhibit optimal properties in minimization problems like the Erds minimum overlap. Unlike previous methods that rely on high-dimensional spatial discretization and local optimization, this strategy reduces complexity by parameterizing the function with two variables:  \n   - **Width** (\\( w \\)) of the central peak,  \n   - **Amplitude** (\\( a \\)) scaling the height.  \n\n   The rationale is that triangular functions are **smooth**, **analytic**, and can model localized structures efficiently. By optimizing these two parameters, we exploit the **convexity** of the objective within a structured manifold, avoiding costly box topology searches. This method addresses the bottleneck of high-dimensional optimization in previous strategies, which could get trapped in narrow plateaus.  \n\n   Limitations include potential suboptimality relative to complex shapes, but the analytic nature ensures **well-behaved gradients** and **robust convergence**. Unlike Fourier or wavelet methods, this avoids numerical spectral inversion risks and remains domain-insensitive through symbolic projection.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   Representation:  \n   \\( h(x) = a \\cdot \\text{triangle}_w(x - 1) \\) over \\([0, 2]\\), where \\(\\text{triangle}_w\\) is a standard triangle extending to \\([-w/2, w/2]\\) after centering at \\(x = 1\\). Explicitly:  \n   \\[\n   \\text{triangle}_w(t) = \n   \\begin{cases} \n   0 & |t|\\geq w/2 \\\\\n   1 - \\frac{2|t|}{w} & |t| \\leq w\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h(x) = \n   \\begin{cases} \n   a \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{2|x-1|}{w}\\right) & |x-1| \\leq w \\\\\n   0 & \\text{else}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Variables: two real parameters \\( w \\in (0, 2] \\), \\( a \\in [0, 1] \\).  \n   Exact Objective:  \n   \\[\n   C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx\n   \\]\n   Enforced via discretization: \\( \\max_{-219 \\leq k \\leq 219} \\text{overlap}[h][k] \\).  \n   Constraints: \\( 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\) is met by triangular construction; normalization holds by parameter choice (\\( \\int_0^2 h(x)  dx = a \\cdot w / 2 \\), so \\( a^* = 2/w \\) to ensure integral=1).\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   Stage 1: **Parameter Initialization**  \n   - Width \\( w \\): Uniform sample from [0.5, 2] with step 0.01.  \n   - Amplitude \\( a = \\min(1, 2/w) \\) ensures \\( h(x) \\leq 1 \\).  \n\n   Stage 2: **Discretization**  \n   - Evaluate \\( h(x_i) \\) for \\( n = 250 \\) points:  \n     \\[\n     x_i = \\frac{4i}{n} \\quad i=0..n-1\n     \\]  \n     \\( h(x_i) = \\max(0, \\min(1, a \\cdot (1 - 2|2x_i - 2|/w))) \\)  \n\n   Stage 3: **Objective Evaluation**  \n   - Compute discrete correlation \\( c[k] = \\sum_{i} h[i] \\cdot (1 - h[i+k]) \\) for \\( |k| < 219 \\), then scale by \\( dx = 2/250 \\).  \n     \\[\n     C_5' = \\max_{k} \\left\\{ \\frac{1}{d} \\sum_{i} h[i] \\cdot (1 - h[i+k]) \\right\\}, \\quad d = \\text{actual shift steps}\n     \\]\n\n   Stage 4: **Optimization Loop**  \n   - **Budgeted Gradient Descent (GD):** For each \\( (w_k, a_k) \\), compute gradients:  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\partial C_5}{\\partial w} \\approx \\frac{C_5(w + \\epsilon) - C_5(w)}{\\epsilon}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial C_5}{\\partial a} \\propto w \\quad (\\text{from parameter coupling})  \n     \\]\n     - Step size \\( \\eta = 0.05 \\), updated by line-search if \\( \\nabla C_5 \\cdot \\partial(x) > 0 \\).  \n   - Early exit if \\( C_5 \\leq 0.380 \\).  \n\n   Stage 5: **Regularity Maintenance**  \n   - Avoid near-singular contours by restarting with:\n     - Uniform prior \\( w \\in [\\text{max}(0.5, w_{\\text{cur}} - 0.1), \\text{min}(2, w_{\\text{cur}} + 0.1)] \\)  \n     - Fix \\( a = 2/w \\) unless near \\( w = 1 \\).\n\n4. **Search Choices and Useful Variations**  \n   Base Configuration:  \n   - Initialize plateau: \\( w_0 = 1.2 \\), \\( a_0 = 2/1.2 \\).  \n   - Max Iterations: 1500, Budget Threshold: 800s.  \n   - Restart Trigger: 3 consecutive increases in \\( C_5 \\) or GD step stalling.  \n\n   Variations:  \n   - **Bimodal Optimization:** Test two peak halos by varying \\( w \\geq 1 \\) and \\( w \\leq 0.8 \\).  \n   - **Spike Handling:** Temporarily reduce \\( a \\) and widen \\( w \\) for avoiding peak-to-peak interference.  \n   - **Amplitude Losses:** When \\( C_5 \\) plateaus, perturb \\( a \\) relative to \\( a = 2/w \\).\n\n5. **Compute Budget and Robustness**  \n   Time Estimation:  \n   - Objective eval: \\( O(n) = 250 \\cdot \\text{shifts} \\) \u2192 55,000 operations per step.  \n   - GD Steps: ~400 budget divisions \u2192 ~90 objective calls.  \n   - Budget Breakpoint: Check residual improvement every 50 steps.  \n\n   Recovery: If budget exhausted, return last stable solution from previous iteration.  \n\n   Handling Edge Cases:  \n   - Invalid shift overlaps: Discard NaNs via bounded polynomial interpolation.  \n   - Boundary parameters: Clip \\( w \\) to [0.01, 2] and \\( a \\) to [0, 1].\n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**  \n   Validation Checks:  \n   - \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) enforced by clamping.  \n   - \\( \\sum h[i] \\cdot dx = 1 \\) guaranteed by \\( a = 2/w \\).  \n   - \\( C_5 \\) re-evaluated with primary code.  \n\n   Output:  \n   - \\( h_{\\text{values}} \\): Triangle function discretized at 250 points.  \n   - \\( C_5 \\): Correlation maximum scaled by \\( dx \\) and rounded to 5 decimals.  \n   - \\( n_{\\text{points}} = 250 \\).  \n\n   Return Format:  \n   [h_values (n_points), C5_bound, n_points]",
